🔹 How NGINX Normally Works (Outside Kubernetes)
If you were not using Kubernetes, you'd configure NGINX manually by writing an nginx.conf 
file like this:

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name myapp.example.com;

    location /service-a {
        proxy_pass http://service-a:8080;
    }

    location /service-b {
        proxy_pass http://service-b:9090;
    }
}


📌 Here, NGINX is manually configured to route /service-a to one backend and /service-b to another.

🔹 How Kubernetes Ingress Works with NGINX
In Kubernetes, we don’t manually configure NGINX. Instead, we:
1️⃣ Deploy the NGINX Ingress Controller inside the cluster.
2️⃣ Define an Ingress Resource (YAML) that specifies the routing rules.
3️⃣ The NGINX Ingress Controller automatically reads the Ingress Resource and updates its internal configuration.
✅ So instead of writing nginx.conf, we write this Ingress Resource in Kubernetes:


apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata:
  name: my-ingress
  annotations:
    kubernetes.io/ingress.class: "nginx"
spec:
  rules:
  - host: myapp.example.com
    http:
      paths:
      - path: /service-a
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: service-a
            port:
              number: 8080
      - path: /service-b
        pathType: Prefix
        backend:
          service:
            name: service-b
            port:
              number: 9090


🔹 What Happens Internally?
1️⃣ The NGINX Ingress Controller runs as a pod inside the cluster.
2️⃣ It listens for any Ingress Resources you create.
3️⃣ When it finds an Ingress Resource, it dynamically updates its routing rules.
4️⃣ Incoming requests are routed based on the rules without manually editing nginx.conf!

💡 Think of the Ingress Resource as a "Kubernetes-friendly way" of configuring NGINX without touching nginx.conf.


🚀 Why Use Ingress Resource Instead of Configuring NGINX Directly?
✅ No need to manually update NGINX config.
✅ Fully automated routing when services scale up/down.
✅ Works natively with Kubernetes services (automatic service discovery).
✅ Easier to manage multiple routes dynamically.

🔹 Why Do We Need a Gateway in Istio Then?
While NGINX Ingress is great for handling basic routing, Istio Gateway is used when you need advanced traffic management (e.g., mTLS, authentication, retries, circuit breaking).

